\chapter{Testing}
\label{Testing}

The tests live in the same files as the code they verify, right after it,
and [[make test]] tangles them into [[tests/test_*.py]] and runs [[pytest]].
This chapter covers what the individual tests share: how a test reaches FBF,
how the tests that talk to FBF can run without it, how we notice that FBF
has changed under us, and how we keep personal data out of what we record.


\section{Two modes, one test}

The tests fall into two groups, by what they need from FBF.
\begin{description}
\item[Offline tests] exercise our own code on inputs written for the test:
  the form parser, the page merging, the HAR shapes.
  They need no network and no credentials.
\item[Service tests] launch FBF from Canvas or send requests to FBF's API,
  and check what comes back.
  They are marked [[vcr]].
\end{description}

The service tests cannot run against a sandbox.
FBF has no test environment that we can reach, and the only way in is the
LTI launch from a real Canvas course (\cref{Launch}).
We use KTH's Canvas course 63874 and its FBF assignment 402780, whose
students are real people.
Yet we want [[make test]] to check the code that talks to FBF offline, without
credentials, and without anyone's data on the screen.

We therefore record the responses once and replay them.
The service tests run in three modes, with the same test code:
\begin{description}
\item[Replay] is the default: [[make test]].
  Each service test gets its responses from a recording, a \emph{cassette}
  in [[tests/cassettes]].
  No request leaves the machine, and no credentials are needed.
  A test without a cassette is skipped, with the reason.
\item[Live] is [[make test PYTESTFLAGS=--live]].
  Every service test runs against Canvas and FBF, and the responses are
  recorded afresh, but into a private directory outside the project, never
  over the cassettes in [[tests/cassettes]].
  Afterwards we compare the structure of the fresh recordings with the ones
  we replay (\cref{Drift}).
  This tells us when FBF has changed its private API, which a replay never
  can; for an API without documentation or announcements, that is the main
  reason to have the live mode at all.
\item[Recording] is [[make test PYTESTFLAGS=--record-mode=once]], which
  records the cassettes that are missing, or [[--record-mode=rewrite]], which
  records them all again.
  It talks to Canvas and FBF like a live run but writes to
  [[tests/cassettes]].
\end{description}
A test that changes data in FBF is marked [[mutates]].
It reaches FBF only when we also give [[--live-writes]]; in replay it
replays like the others.
None of the tests in this version writes, but the rule is in place before
the first one that does.

The cassettes are not in the repository.
Even pseudonymized, they describe a real course: who viewed which activity
and when, linked by FBF's identifiers, and whoever holds the key can undo the
pseudonyms.
They stay on the machine that recorded them, and [[tests/.gitignore]] keeps
them out of git.
Anyone with a Canvas login to a course with an FBF assignment can record their
own (\cref{RecordingOwn}); without cassettes, the service tests are skipped
and the offline tests still run.


\section{The shared test configuration}

[[pytest]] reads command-line options and shared fixtures from a file
[[conftest.py]] in the test directory, which we tangle from here.
The recording itself is done by the [[pytest-recording]] plugin, which wraps
[[vcrpy]].
A test marked [[@pytest.mark.vcr]] gets a cassette named after the test,
and [[--record-mode]] chooses between replaying and recording.
We add the live mode, the launch for the tests, and the privacy protection.

The scrubbing and the drift comparison come from [[vcr_support.py]].
It is not ours: it is a helper from the [[testing]] skill for Claude Code,
committed unchanged in [[tests/]] so that it can be updated from its source
(the copy is the skill's [[assets/vcr_support.py]] of October 2026, which is
newer than the copy in [[ladok3]], whose test setup this chapter follows).
Everything that is specific to Canvas and FBF we pass to it from here, rather
than editing the copy.

Unlike the other modules, [[conftest.py]] is tangled from \emph{all} chapters,
as [[schema.py]] is (\cref{Schema}): each chapter that declares names FBF must
have can also say how to fetch an example of them, in a chunk
[[<<example fetchers>>]] (\cref{Examples}).
Since tangling from several files joins all chunks of the same name, the
chunks of this chapter have names of their own, not the [[<<constants>>]] or
[[<<functions>>]] that every chapter has.
<<[[conftest.py]]>>=
