@

Replay must never reach the network, not even by mistake.
In [[pyproject.toml]] we therefore give [[pytest]] the options
[[--block-network --allowed-hosts=127.0.0.1,::1,localhost]]: any request to
another host then fails, unless a live run or a recording turns the block off.


\section{Choosing the mode}

The two live options are separate because they carry different risks.
A read test only looks at the data, while a write test changes it.
<<pytest options>>=
def pytest_addoption(parser):
    group = parser.getgroup("fbf")
    group.addoption(
        "--live", action="store_true",
        help="run the service tests against Canvas and FBF, record into a "
             "private directory and compare with the cassettes")
    group.addoption(
        "--live-writes", action="store_true",
        help="with --live or --record-mode, also run the tests that change "
             "data in FBF")
    group.addoption(
        "--live-dir", default=None,
        help="where --live records, as --live-dir=PATH "
             "(default: a private directory under the temporary directory)")
@

A live run is a recording that goes elsewhere.
We set the record mode to [[all]], so every request goes to the services,
and turn the network block off.
We have to set the options themselves rather than the fixtures, because
[[pytest-recording]] reads its network block from the options.
Recording needs the pseudonymization key; without it we refuse to start
rather than record anyone's data in clear.
<<pytest configuration>>=
def pytest_configure(config):
    config.addinivalue_line(
        "markers",
        "mutates: the test changes data in FBF; reaches FBF only with "
        "--live-writes")
    config._recorded_dirs = set()
    if config.getoption("--live"):
        config.option.record_mode = "all"
        config.option.block_network = False
        config._live_dir = live_directory(config)
        prepare_live_dir(config._live_dir, Path(str(config.rootpath)).resolve())
    if record_mode_of(config) != "none" \
            and not os.environ.get(vcr_support.KEY_ENV):
        pytest.exit(f"refusing to record: set {vcr_support.KEY_ENV} so that "
                    "personal data is pseudonymized", returncode=2)


def record_mode_of(config):
    """Return the record mode, `none` meaning replay."""
    return config.getoption("--record-mode") or "none"
@

\subsection{Testing the configuration in inner runs}
\label{InnerRuns}

Much of this configuration acts on the run as a whole: it refuses to start,
skips tests, stops the run or fails it at the end.
We test that by running [[pytest]] itself, with this configuration, on small
test files of our own, through the [[pytester]] fixture.
Such an inner run must never reach Canvas or FBF, whatever mode we put it in
and whatever the machine has stored: [[make test]] runs these tests, and on
a machine with a [[canvaslms]] login a recording or live inner run would
otherwise launch for real.
The inner tests mostly replace the launch fixture, but a renamed fixture
would quietly undo that.
So every inner run gets a configuration that cannot log in: we append a
[[canvas_credentials]] fixture (\cref{TestLaunch}) that finds no
credentials and notes in the environment that it was asked, which lets a
test check that an inner run did not even try.
<<test functions>>=
INNER_GUARD = """

@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def canvas_credentials():
    \"\"\"An inner run never logs in, whatever this machine has stored.\"\"\"
    def none_stored():
        os.environ["FBF_INNER_ASKED_FOR_CREDENTIALS"] = "yes"
        raise CredentialsMissing("the inner run never logs in")
    return none_stored
"""

def make_inner_conftest(pytester, monkeypatch):
  """Give the inner run this configuration, unable to log in."""
  monkeypatch.setenv("FBF_INNER_ASKED_FOR_CREDENTIALS", "no")
  pytester.makeconftest(file_text(conftest.__file__) + INNER_GUARD)

def file_text(path):
  with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as file:
    return file.read()
@

The first inner run checks the refusal to record without a key, on an empty
test directory.
The run must stop before it collects anything, so nothing can reach Canvas
or FBF.
[[pytester]] cannot run in its own process a [[pytest]] that exits while it
configures itself, so this inner run gets a process of its own, and with it
a copy of [[vcr_support.py]], which the configuration imports from its own
directory.
<<test functions>>=
def test_recording_without_a_key_is_refused(pytester, monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.delenv("TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY", raising=False)
  make_inner_conftest(pytester, monkeypatch)
  pytester.makepyfile(vcr_support=file_text(conftest.vcr_support.__file__))
  result = pytester.runpytest_subprocess(
    "--record-mode=once", "-p", "no:cacheprovider")
  assert result.ret == 2
  result.stderr.fnmatch_lines(["*refusing to record*"])
@

The live recordings hold real people's data, pseudonymized, so they go in a
private directory outside the repository rather than in the shared temporary
directory.
The directory name includes a hash of the project's path, so two checkouts
(such as two git worktrees) don't share one.
<<pytest configuration>>=
def live_directory(config):
    """Return the directory that a live run records into."""
    root = Path(str(config.rootpath))
    tag = hashlib.sha256(str(root.resolve()).encode()).hexdigest()[:10]
    return Path(config.getoption("--live-dir")
                or vcr_support.private_dir()
                / f"{root.name}-{tag}-live-cassettes").resolve()
@

A live run starts with an empty directory, so that recordings from an earlier
run can't hide that a request is no longer made.
Emptying a directory is dangerous if the path is mistyped, so we only empty a
directory that an earlier live run wrote, as shown by a marker file, and we
never accept a directory that contains the project.
<<constants for the tests>>=
LIVE_MARKER_FILE = ".pytest-live-cassettes"
<<pytest configuration>>=
def prepare_live_dir(live_dir, root):
    """Empty `live_dir` for a live run, if an earlier live run made it."""
    if live_dir == root or live_dir in root.parents:
        pytest.exit(f"refusing --live-dir={live_dir}: it contains the project",
                    returncode=2)
    if live_dir.exists() and any(live_dir.iterdir()):
        if not (live_dir / LIVE_MARKER_FILE).exists():
            pytest.exit(f"refusing to empty --live-dir={live_dir}: it has "
                        "files that an earlier live run did not write",
                        returncode=2)
        shutil.rmtree(live_dir)
    live_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (live_dir / LIVE_MARKER_FILE).write_text(
        "written by pytest --live; safe to delete\n")
@

Let's verify that a live run empties only its own directory.
<<test functions>>=
def test_prepare_live_dir_empties_only_its_own(tmp_path):
  project = tmp_path / "project"
  live = tmp_path / "live"
  conftest.prepare_live_dir(live, project)
  (live / "old.yaml").write_text("from an earlier live run")
  conftest.prepare_live_dir(live, project)
  assert not (live / "old.yaml").exists()

def test_prepare_live_dir_refuses_foreign_files(tmp_path):
  live = tmp_path / "live"
  live.mkdir()
  (live / "precious.txt").write_text("not ours")
  with pytest.raises(pytest.exit.Exception):
    conftest.prepare_live_dir(live, tmp_path / "project")
  assert (live / "precious.txt").exists()

def test_prepare_live_dir_refuses_the_project(tmp_path):
  with pytest.raises(pytest.exit.Exception):
    conftest.prepare_live_dir(tmp_path, tmp_path / "project")
@

A [[mutates]] test is skipped when it would reach FBF, in a live run or
while recording, unless we asked for writes.
In replay it runs: then its writes go to the cassette, not to FBF.
<<collection rules>>=
def pytest_collection_modifyitems(config, items):
    talks_to_fbf = record_mode_of(config) != "none"
    if not talks_to_fbf or config.getoption("--live-writes"):
        return
    skip_writes = pytest.mark.skip(
        reason="changes data in FBF; add --live-writes")
    for item in items:
        if "mutates" in item.keywords:
            item.add_marker(skip_writes)
@

Let's verify the rule in each mode, recording included, in an inner run.
Its tests replace the launch fixture (\cref{TestLaunch}), so that recording
can go on without a launch; we check that the inner run did not even ask
for credentials.
<<test functions>>=
INNER_TESTS = """
import pytest

@pytest.fixture(scope="session", autouse=True)
def launched():
  return "the inner run never launches"

@pytest.mark.mutates
def test_writes():
  pass

def test_reads():
  pass
"""

@pytest.mark.parametrize("options,passed,skipped", [
  ([], 2, 0),
  (["--live"], 1, 1),
  (["--live", "--live-writes"], 2, 0),
  (["--record-mode=once"], 1, 1),
])
def test_mutates_tests_reach_fbf_only_with_live_writes(
    pytester, monkeypatch, tmp_path, options, passed, skipped):
  monkeypatch.setenv("TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY", "a key for the inner run")
  make_inner_conftest(pytester, monkeypatch)
  pytester.makepyfile(test_inner=INNER_TESTS)
  result = pytester.runpytest_inprocess(
    *options, f"--live-dir={tmp_path / 'live'}", "-p", "no:cacheprovider")
  result.assert_outcomes(passed=passed, skipped=skipped)
  assert os.environ["FBF_INNER_ASKED_FOR_CREDENTIALS"] == "no"
@


\section{The launch for the tests}
\label{TestLaunch}

Every service test needs FBF tokens, and the only way to get them is the
launch of \cref{Launch}, which takes half a dozen requests through Canvas and
FBF.
We launch once per run, with the Canvas API token that [[canvaslms]] stores,
and let all tests share the tokens: they are the user's, not the launched
activity's, so one launch serves every test.
The test target is a pair of constants.
<<constants for the tests>>=
CANVAS_URL = "https://canvas.kth.se"
COURSE_ID = 63874
ASSIGNMENT_ID = 402780
@

\subsection{Launching before any cassette opens}

The shared launch must not end up in a test's cassette.
Which test happens to run first is an accident of ordering, and that test's
cassette would then hold a launch that the same test, run alone in replay,
would not make.
[[pytest-recording]] opens a test's cassette in a function-scoped fixture,
and [[pytest]] sets up session-scoped fixtures before function-scoped ones,
so a session-scoped fixture that launches does so outside every test's
cassette.
It must be [[autouse]], because a fixture that a test asks for by name may
be set up after the cassette is already open.

When there are no stored Canvas credentials, the service tests are skipped
rather than failed, since the problem is the machine's set-up, not the code.
The fixture itself must not skip, though: skipping an [[autouse]] fixture
would skip the offline tests too.
It returns the reason instead, and the fixtures that the service tests ask
for skip with it.
Any other error, such as a [[LaunchError]], is left to fail, because it may
well be a bug in the launch or a change at Canvas or FBF.
<<test fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def recording(record_mode):
    """True when the tests talk to Canvas and FBF: live or recording."""
    return record_mode != "none"


@pytest.fixture(scope="session", autouse=True)
def launched(recording, pytestconfig, pseudonymizer, canvas_credentials):
    """The launch that the tests share when they talk to FBF, or the reason
    why there is none.  Launches before any cassette opens."""
    if not recording:
        return "replaying"
    cassette = recording_dir(pytestconfig, LAUNCH_CASSETTE.parent) \
        / LAUNCH_CASSETTE.name
    return log_in_for_tests(
        lambda: launch_for_tests(cassette, pseudonymizer, canvas_credentials),
        into_project=not pytestconfig.getoption("--live"))
@

Skipping is right for a live run, which records into a directory of its own.
It is wrong when we record into the project's cassettes.
With [[--record-mode=rewrite]], [[pytest-recording]] deletes a test's
cassette when it opens it, and that happens before the [[fbf]] fixture
learns that there is no launch and skips.
A run without credentials would then delete the very cassettes that it was
meant to renew, and still exit successfully, reporting only skips.
So when we record into the project, missing credentials stop the whole run,
here, before any cassette opens.

One kind of error we always let through: [[RecursionError]].
It subclasses [[RuntimeError]], so a broad handler would take a launch that
loops for a missing login; [[ladok3]] once lost its cassettes that way.
Our handler catches only [[CredentialsMissing]], which makes that mistake
impossible, and a test below keeps it so.
<<test fixtures>>=
class CredentialsMissing(Exception):
    """There is no Canvas login to launch FBF with."""


def log_in_for_tests(log_in, into_project):
    """Return what `log_in()` returns, or the reason why there is no login.
    Stops the run instead when we record into the project's cassettes,
    which a skipped test could delete."""
    try:
        return log_in()
    except CredentialsMissing as err:
        if into_project:
            pytest.exit(f"cannot launch FBF, so nothing is recorded: {err}",
                        returncode=1)
        return f"cannot launch FBF: {err}"
@

Let's verify the three outcomes with a login that fails the way missing
credentials do, and with one that fails the way a looping login does.
We inject the failures: the real login would need credentials and Canvas.
<<test functions>>=
def no_stored_credentials():
  raise conftest.CredentialsMissing("no Canvas login")

def test_missing_credentials_stop_a_recording_into_the_project():
  with pytest.raises(pytest.exit.Exception):
    conftest.log_in_for_tests(no_stored_credentials, into_project=True)

def test_missing_credentials_skip_the_service_tests_of_a_live_run():
  reason = conftest.log_in_for_tests(no_stored_credentials,
                                     into_project=False)
  assert "no Canvas login" in reason

def test_a_login_that_loops_is_a_bug_not_a_missing_login():
  def looping_login():
    raise RecursionError("maximum recursion depth exceeded")

  with pytest.raises(RecursionError):
    conftest.log_in_for_tests(looping_login, into_project=False)
