The credentials are those of [[canvaslms]], which keeps them in the keyring,
the environment or its configuration.
[[canvaslms]] is not a dependency of [[fbf]] itself, so a machine without it
simply has no credentials.
We take only the token: the cassettes are recorded against [[CANVAS_URL]],
and replay must ask for the same host.
The function that reads them is what the fixture [[canvas_credentials]]
gives; the inner runs that test this configuration replace it with one
that never finds any.
<<test fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def canvas_credentials():
    """The function that returns the Canvas token to launch with, or raises
    `CredentialsMissing`."""
    return canvas_token_for_tests


def canvas_token_for_tests():
    """Return the Canvas API token that `canvaslms` stores."""
    try:
        from canvaslms.cli.login import load_credentials
    except ImportError as err:
        raise CredentialsMissing("canvaslms is not installed") from err
    _, token = load_credentials({})
    if not token:
        raise CredentialsMissing("no Canvas login; run `canvaslms login`")
    return token
@

\subsection{Replaying the launch}

A test in replay needs tokens too, or rather it needs an [[FBFLogin]]: the
tokens can be anything, since the cassettes keep no [[Authorization]]
header, but the API URL and the UUIDs of the course and the activity must be
the ones the requests were recorded with.
We could write those into the code, but they would go stale with every new
recording.
Instead the shared launch is itself recorded, into a cassette of its own,
and replay replays it: the replayed launch runs our real launch code and
ends with the same API URL and UUIDs, and with placeholder tokens.

The live launch records into a partial file and replaces the old cassette
only when the launch succeeded.
In [[all]] mode [[vcrpy]] keeps the interactions of an existing cassette that
the new run did not repeat, so writing over the old file directly would mix
two launches; and a launch that failed halfway must not destroy the one that
worked.
<<constants for the tests>>=
TESTS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
LAUNCH_CASSETTE = TESTS_DIR / "cassettes" / "session" / "launch.yaml"
REPLAY_TOKEN = "replay-token"
<<test fixtures>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class Launched:
    """The live launch that the tests share."""

    canvas_token: str
    login: object


def launch_for_tests(cassette, pseudonymizer, canvas_credentials):
    """Launch FBF for real, recording the launch into `cassette`."""
    canvas_token = canvas_credentials()
    cassette.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    partial = cassette.with_suffix(".partial.yaml")
    try:
        with launch_recorder(pseudonymizer).use_cassette(
                str(partial), record_mode="all"):
            login = launch(CANVAS_URL, canvas_token, COURSE_ID, ASSIGNMENT_ID)
        partial.replace(cassette)
    finally:
        partial.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return Launched(canvas_token, login)


def launch_recorder(pseudonymizer):
    """Return a `vcrpy` recorder configured like the tests' cassettes."""
    return vcrpy.VCR(**pseudonymizer.vcr_config())
@

The replayed launch is made once per run, when the first test needs it.
That is inside the test's own cassette, which is already open; [[vcrpy]]
lets the launch cassette take over for the duration of the launch and then
hands back.
In replay the network is blocked anyway, so a launch request that the
cassette lacks fails instead of reaching Canvas.
<<test fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def replayed_login(pseudonymizer):
    """The `FBFLogin` of the recorded launch, replayed."""
    if not LAUNCH_CASSETTE.exists():
        pytest.skip("no recorded launch; record with --record-mode=once")
    with launch_recorder(pseudonymizer).use_cassette(
            str(LAUNCH_CASSETTE), record_mode="none"):
        return launch(CANVAS_URL, REPLAY_TOKEN, COURSE_ID, ASSIGNMENT_ID)
@

\subsection{What a service test asks for}

The tests ask for [[fbf]], a client for FBF, or for [[canvas_token]], if they
launch themselves.
They get whatever fits the mode.
In replay, a test without a cassette is skipped: without one, its first
request would only fail on the network block.
A test that uses these fixtures must be marked [[vcr]], though, or it is never
recorded: it would be skipped in replay for ever and talk to FBF unrecorded
in a live run, outside the drift check.
We fail such a test rather than let it skip quietly.
<<test fixtures>>=
def require_service(request, recording, launched):
    """Fail, skip or go on with a service test, as its mode requires.
    Returns the live launch, or `None` in replay."""
    if request.node.get_closest_marker("vcr") is None:
        pytest.fail("a test that talks to Canvas or FBF must be marked vcr")
    if recording:
        if isinstance(launched, str):
            pytest.skip(launched)
        return launched
    cassette = Path(request.getfixturevalue("vcr_cassette_dir")) \
        / f"{request.getfixturevalue('default_cassette_name')}.yaml"
    if not cassette.exists():
        pytest.skip("no recording; record it with --record-mode=once, "
                    "or run with --live")
    return None
