Each test gets a client of its own, so that no state carries over from one
test to the next.
What the clients share differs by mode.
In a live run they share the launch's [[FBFLogin]]: when a test renews the
tokens, FBF also replaces the refresh token, so the old one may no longer
work, and the next test must see the new tokens.
In replay each client gets a copy of the replayed login, so that a test that
changes it changes nothing for the next.
The renewal test in \cref{API}, for one, sets an expired access token and
lets the renewal replace it.
Shared, the next test would start from whatever that left behind instead of
from the replayed launch.
No test fails because of it today, since replay does not match on the
[[Authorization]] header; the copy keeps it that way when a test starts to
depend on the token.
