\subsection{Pseudonymization}
\label{Pseudonymization}

The responses we record are about real people.
[[vcr_support]] scrubs each response before it is written: secrets such as
cookies and tokens are removed, names, email addresses and usernames are
replaced by pseudonyms, and free text is replaced wholesale, since prose can
name anyone.
The pseudonyms are keyed hashes, so the same person gets the same pseudonym
in every cassette, and only someone with the key can link a pseudonym to a
person.
The key, in the environment variable [[TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY]], must therefore
never be committed; it lives in the keyring (\cref{RecordingOwn}).
It must also stay the same between recordings, or every pseudonym changes and
a re-recording becomes impossible to review.

[[vcr_support]] knows Canvas's field names, which are its defaults.
FBF needs more, for four reasons, which the rest of this section takes in
turn.
\begin{enumerate}
\item FBF writes its attribute names in kebab-case.
\item FBF is a JSON:API, where a person's name sits in an [[attributes]]
  object that says nothing about being a person.
\item The launch passes its secrets in URLs, forms and JSON values, not only
  in the headers and query parameters that [[vcr_support]] filters.
\item The launch pages are HTML, which [[vcr_support]] does not scrub by key.
\end{enumerate}
We teach [[vcr_support]]'s pseudonymizer these in a subclass.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
class FBFPseudonymizer(vcr_support.Pseudonymizer):
    """The pseudonymizer of `vcr_support`, taught Canvas's and FBF's ways."""

    def __init__(self, key, originals_path=None):
        super().__init__(
            key, originals_path,
            name_keys=FBF_NAME_KEYS, email_keys=FBF_EMAIL_KEYS,
            login_keys=FBF_LOGIN_KEYS, free_text_keys=FBF_FREE_TEXT_KEYS,
            url_identity_keys=FBF_URL_IDENTITY_KEYS)

    <<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>
@

The pseudonymizer notes every original value it replaced, in a private file
outside the repository, so that the scan after the run (\cref{Drift}) can
look for any of them that slipped through elsewhere.
In replay nothing is written, and any key will do, since nothing is
pseudonymized.
The [[vcrpy]] configuration comes from the pseudonymizer, as in
[[vcr_support]]; it matches requests on their bodies too, so that a write
test in replay must send exactly what was recorded.
<<test fixtures>>=
