\subsubsection{Field names in kebab-case}

[[vcr_support]] matches keys such as [[first_name]] and [[avatar_url]].
FBF calls the same things [[first-name]] and [[avatar-url]], so for each of
[[vcr_support]]'s key lists we add the kebab-case spelling of every key.
On top of that come FBF's own fields:
\begin{description}
\item[[[address]] ] is the address of an [[emails]] resource.
  [[vcr_support]] counts it as a postal address and replaces it with a
  placeholder; as an email key it gets a pseudonymous email address instead,
  which keeps the type of value and is the same in every cassette.
\item[[[body]], [[body-rendered]] ] are the texts of comments and answers,
  and [[body-rendered]] their HTML.
\item[[[solution]], [[solution-rendered]] ] are a teacher's model answer.
\item[[[description]], [[description-rendered]] ] are the instructions of an
  activity or a task.
  They are written by teachers, not students, but they are free text and
  may name anyone.
\end{description}
Titles we keep: the [[title]] of an activity is course content, it names the
assignment rather than a person, and the tests may want to read it.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
def kebab(keys):
    """Return `keys` together with their kebab-case spellings."""
    return set(keys) | {key.replace("_", "-") for key in keys}


FBF_NAME_KEYS = kebab(vcr_support.NAME_KEYS)
FBF_EMAIL_KEYS = kebab(vcr_support.EMAIL_KEYS) | {"address"}
FBF_LOGIN_KEYS = kebab(vcr_support.LOGIN_KEYS)
FBF_URL_IDENTITY_KEYS = kebab(vcr_support.URL_IDENTITY_KEYS)
FBF_FREE_TEXT_KEYS = kebab(vcr_support.FREE_TEXT_KEYS) | {
    "body-rendered", "solution", "solution-rendered",
    "description-rendered"}
@

\subsubsection{People in JSON:API}

[[vcr_support]] replaces a [[name]] only in an object that looks like a
person: one that also has an email or a login, or that sits under a key such
as [[user]] or [[author]].
Otherwise every course, file and activity name would be taken for a person's.
In JSON:API, a user is
\begin{center}
[[{"type": "users", "id": ..., "attributes": {"name": ..., ...}}]]
\end{center}
and its [[attributes]] object has neither: the [[name]] would pass through.
What does say \enquote{person} is the [[type]] of the resource, so for the
resource types in [[FBF_PERSON_TYPES]] we scrub the attributes as if they sat
under [[user]].
(The emails of a user are separate [[emails]] resources, which the
[[address]] key above covers.)

A user also has a [[zendesk-hash]], which FBF's web app hands to its help
desk to prove who the user is: a credential, not data.
The launch and the token renewal carry tokens as JSON values too.
Such values are replaced by a placeholder wherever their key occurs.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
FBF_PERSON_TYPES = {"users"}
FBF_SECRET_KEYS = kebab({
    "access_token", "refresh_token", "id_token", "session_token",
    "login_hint", "lti_message_hint", "authenticity_token", "csrf_token",
    "secure_params", "zendesk_hash"})


def is_person_resource(obj):
    """True if `obj` is a JSON:API resource that describes a person."""
    kind = obj.get("type")
    return (isinstance(kind, str) and kind in FBF_PERSON_TYPES
            and "id" in obj and isinstance(obj.get("attributes"), dict))


def redact_secret(key, value):
    """Return a placeholder for `value` if `key` names a secret."""
    if str(key).lower() in FBF_SECRET_KEYS and isinstance(value, str) and value:
        return vcr_support.SECRET_PLACEHOLDER
    return value
@

[[vcr_support]] walks the JSON in a method [[_collect]], which takes the key
that an object sits under; we step in before it.
Overriding a private method ties us to the internals of [[vcr_support]], so
the tests below check the result, not the mechanism: if a new copy of
[[vcr_support]] works differently, they fail.
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def _collect(self, obj, parent=None):
    if isinstance(obj, dict):
        obj = {key: redact_secret(key, value) for key, value in obj.items()}
        if parent != "relationships":
            obj = {key: self._free_text(key, value)
                   for key, value in obj.items()}
        if is_person_resource(obj):
            return {key: self._collect(
                        value, "user" if key == "attributes" else str(key).lower())
                    for key, value in obj.items()}
    return super()._collect(obj, parent)
@

