\subsubsection{Titles in Canvas objects}

The first recording replaced the title of an assignment in one place and
kept it in another: as the [[name]] of the Canvas assignment it became a
pseudonym, and its words were noted as originals; as the [[title]] of the
FBF activity it was kept, and the scan after the run reported it.
The Canvas assignment looked like a person to [[vcr_support]], because it
has a key [[integration_id]], one of the login keys, which marks an object
as a person's; Canvas sets that key on many objects, and on an assignment
it is [[null]].
We keep titles, as course content, so the fix is to the test for a person:
an identity field marks a person only if it holds a value.
An empty one identifies no one.
Under a key that names a person, such as [[user]], an object is still a
person's regardless.
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def _is_person(self, obj, parent):
    filled = {key: value for key, value in obj.items()
              if value not in (None, "", [], {})}
    return super()._is_person(filled, parent)
@

