\subsubsection{Which free text to look for}
\label{FreeTextNoted}

Every free text that the pseudonymizer replaces is noted as an original, so
that the scan after the run can look for it elsewhere.
The scan looks for whole words, and some free texts are a single word: the
answer to a true-or-false question is [[true]] or [[false]], other answers
are [[yes]] or a short word in Swedish, and a Delta's formatting holds words
such as [[bullet]].
The first recording noted all of them, and the scan found [[true]] and
[[false]] in every JSON body, as literals, some twenty thousand times.
A single word cannot be told from the same word elsewhere, and it says
nothing about anyone on its own.
So we still replace every free text, but note only those of two words or
more, which a coincidence will not reproduce; for the words of a Delta, the
same goes for each of its strings.
Names are not affected: the pseudonymizer notes those, and their parts, as
it always did.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
def identifying(text):
    """True if the free text `text` is worth looking for elsewhere."""
    return len(text.split()) >= 2
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def _free_text(self, key, value):
    """Return the placeholder for the free text `value` under `key`, noting
    the text if it is worth looking for."""
    name = str(key).lower()
    if (name not in self.free_text_keys or name in self.keep_text
            or not value or isinstance(value, (int, float, bool))
            or (isinstance(value, str) and is_ours(value))):
        return value
    self._log(*[text for text in strings_in(value) if identifying(text)])
    return (text_placeholder(value) if isinstance(value, (dict, list))
            else vcr_support.FREE_TEXT_PLACEHOLDER)
@

