\subsubsection{Secrets in URLs}

The launch passes one-time secrets in URLs.
Canvas's sessionless launch URL (hop~0) carries a [[verifier]] and the
[[session_token]] that logs the session in, inside a JSON value; FBF's
redirect to Canvas (hop~2) carries [[login_hint]], [[lti_message_hint]],
[[state]] and [[nonce]], in a [[Location]] header.
[[vcrpy]] removes secret parameters from the URLs of the recorded requests,
but it never looks at response bodies or headers.
We extend its list of parameters and, in every string that the pseudonymizer
scrubs, replace the value of such a parameter with the placeholder.
We keep the parameter itself: hop~0 checks that the URL has a
[[session_token]], and the replayed launch must pass that check.
In replay the placeholder then appears in the requests that follow the URL,
and since [[vcrpy]] removes the parameter from them before matching, as it
did when recording, they still match.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
SECRET_PARAMS = vcr_support.SECRET_QUERY_PARAMS + [
    "login_hint", "lti_message_hint", "state", "nonce", "verifier",
    "authenticity_token"]
URL_SECRET = re.compile(
    r"(?i)([?&;])(" + "|".join(map(re.escape, SECRET_PARAMS)) + r")="
    r"([^&#\s\"'<>\\]+)")


def redact_url_secrets(text):
    """Replace the values of secret parameters in the URLs in `text`."""
    return URL_SECRET.sub(
        lambda match: f"{match[1]}{match[2]}={vcr_support.SECRET_PLACEHOLDER}",
        text)
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def scrub_text(self, text):
    return redact_url_secrets(super().scrub_text(text))


def vcr_config(self):
    config = super().vcr_config()
    config["filter_query_parameters"] = SECRET_PARAMS
    config["filter_post_data_parameters"] = SECRET_PARAMS
    return config
@

Let's verify it on a sessionless launch response with made-up values.
