\subsubsection{The launch pages}

Hops 1 to 3 answer with HTML pages: Canvas's assignment page, the form that
carries the [[id_token]], and FBF's web app.
[[vcr_support]] scrubs HTML only for the names and emails that it has already
learned from JSON, and a Canvas page holds much more: the logged-in user's
name and settings in its JavaScript, CSRF tokens, links.
Rather than find and remove all that, we keep only what the launch reads: the
forms with their fields, and the [[<meta>]] tags.
Keeping a list of what may stay is the safer direction: when Canvas adds
something to its pages, it is dropped rather than recorded.
We build the reduced page, the \emph{skeleton}, with the parser of the launch
itself (\cref{Launch}), so it holds exactly what the launch can see.

In the skeleton, a form field keeps its value only if it is in
[[KEPT_FORM_FIELDS]], which names the fields of the two launch forms that
identify the tool and the course rather than the user or the session; the
other fields keep their names but get the placeholder.
The same goes for the [[<meta>]] tags, of which the launch reads only
[[api-url]].
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
KEPT_FORM_FIELDS = {
    "canvas_environment", "canvas_region", "client_id", "grant_type", "iss",
    "lti_deployment_id", "lti_storage_target", "target_link_uri", "utf8"}
KEPT_META = {"api-url"}


def html_skeleton(text):
    """Return the forms and meta tags of the HTML page `text`, with the
    values that may identify someone replaced."""
    page = parse_page(text)
    lines = ["<!DOCTYPE html>", "<html><head>"]
    lines += [tag("meta", name=name, content=kept(name, content, KEPT_META))
              for name, content in page.meta.items()]
    lines.append("</head><body>")
    for form in page.forms:
        lines.append(tag("form", action=redact_url_secrets(form.action),
                         method=form.method))
        lines += [tag("input", type="hidden", name=name,
                      value=kept(name, value, KEPT_FORM_FIELDS))
                  for name, value in form.fields.items()]
        lines.append("</form>")
    lines.append("</body></html>")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def kept(name, value, names):
    """Return `value` if `name` is in `names`, else the placeholder."""
    return (value or "") if name in names else vcr_support.SECRET_PLACEHOLDER


def tag(element, **attributes):
    """Return an HTML start tag."""
    return f"<{element}" + "".join(
        f' {name}="{html.escape(value)}"'
        for name, value in attributes.items()) + ">"
@

The forms are submitted, so the same fields appear again in the bodies of
the requests that follow.
There we treat them the same way: a field outside [[KEPT_FORM_FIELDS]] gets
the placeholder.
This also makes replay work.
Requests match on their bodies, and in replay the launch submits the
skeleton's placeholders where the recording submitted the real values; since
[[vcrpy]] runs this scrubbing on a request before matching it, as it did
when recording, both become the same body.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
def is_form(request):
    """True if `request` has a form-encoded body."""
    return "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" in (
        request.headers.get("Content-Type") or "")


def keep_form_fields(body):
    """Return the form-encoded `body` with the values that may identify
    someone replaced."""
    text = body.decode("utf-8") if isinstance(body, bytes) else body
    fields = urllib.parse.parse_qsl(text, keep_blank_values=True)
    return urllib.parse.urlencode(
        [(name, kept(name, value, KEPT_FORM_FIELDS))
         for name, value in fields]).encode("utf-8")
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def before_record_request(self, request):
    if request.body and is_form(request):
        request.body = keep_form_fields(request.body)
    return super().before_record_request(request)
@

The response headers get the same treatment as the pages: we keep the few
that a client needs, and [[vcr_support]] then replaces the values of the
cookies and scrubs the [[Location]].
The others would only tell a reader about the session (Canvas sends request
and session identifiers), and none of our code reads them.

The cookies also lose their lifetime.
FBF's [[access_token]] cookie expires after 30~minutes, and [[requests]]
honours that in replay as a browser would: half an hour after the recording,
the replayed cookie has expired before it arrives, [[requests]] drops it,
and hop~3 of the replayed launch fails for want of an [[access_token]].
A replay runs at any time after the recording, so a lifetime measured from
the recording means nothing there.
We therefore remove [[Expires]] and [[Max-Age]], which makes every replayed
cookie a session cookie.
The price is that a cookie that the service deletes, by giving it a
lifetime in the past, stays set in replay; our code looks up cookies only by
name, after the launch has set them, so that does not matter to it.
[[vcrpy]] also runs this hook on the responses it loads from a cassette, so
cassettes recorded before this rule are fixed as they are read.
<<scrubbing for FBF>>=
KEPT_RESPONSE_HEADERS = {"content-type", "content-length", "location",
                         "set-cookie"}
COOKIE_LIFETIME = re.compile(r"(?i);\s*(?:expires|max-age)=[^;]*")
<<[[FBFPseudonymizer]] methods>>=
def before_record_response(self, response):
    headers = response.get("headers", {})
    for name in list(headers):
        if name.lower() not in KEPT_RESPONSE_HEADERS:
            del headers[name]
        elif name.lower() == "set-cookie":
            headers[name] = [COOKIE_LIFETIME.sub("", value)
                             for value in headers[name]]
    content_type = " ".join(
        value for name, values in headers.items()
        if name.lower() == "content-type" for value in values)
    body = response.get("body", {})
    if "text/html" in content_type and body.get("string"):
        text = body["string"]
        was_bytes = isinstance(text, bytes)
        if was_bytes:
            text = text.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
        text = html_skeleton(text)
        body["string"] = text.encode("utf-8") if was_bytes else text
    return super().before_record_response(response)
@

Let's verify the skeleton on a page like Canvas's, with made-up values: a
script naming the user, a CSRF token, the tool form with an identity hint,
and the API URL.
The launch must find the same form and API URL in the skeleton as in the
page, but none of the other values.
<<test functions>>=
CANVAS_PAGE = """<!DOCTYPE html><html><head>
<meta name="csrf-token" content="Q1w2e3r4t5y6u7i8o9p0">
<meta name="api-url" content="https://api.example.com">
<script>ENV = {"current_user": {"display_name": "Anna Andersson"}}</script>
</head><body>
<form action="https://api.example.com/v1/lti/login" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="iss" value="https://canvas.instructure.com">
  <input type="hidden" name="login_hint" value="a1b2c3d4e5">
</form></body></html>"""

def recorded_response(body, **headers):
  headers = {"Content-Type": ["text/html; charset=utf-8"], **headers}
  return {"status": {"code": 200, "message": "OK"}, "headers": headers,
          "body": {"string": body.encode("utf-8")}}

def test_recordings_keep_only_the_skeleton_of_pages(scrubber):
  response = scrubber.before_record_response(
    recorded_response(CANVAS_PAGE, **{"X-Session-Id": ["s3ss10n"]}))
  assert list(response["headers"]) == ["Content-Type"]
  text = response["body"]["string"].decode("utf-8")
  for value in ("Anna", "Q1w2e3r4t5y6u7i8o9p0", "a1b2c3d4e5"):
    assert value not in text, value
  page = conftest.parse_page(text)
  form = page.forms[0]
  assert form.action == "https://api.example.com/v1/lti/login"
  assert form.fields == {"iss": "https://canvas.instructure.com",
                         "login_hint": "redacted"}
  assert page.meta == {"csrf-token": "redacted",
                       "api-url": "https://api.example.com"}
@

The redirects carry their secrets in the [[Location]] header and their
