\section{An example of every type we depend on}
\label{Examples}

\Cref{Schema} declares the names of attributes and relationships that our
code reads, type by type, and the contract test there checks each of them
against FBF.
To check a type, the test needs a resource of that type, and how to find one
differs from type to type: a placement hangs off the launched activity, a
comment off a video activity somewhere in the course.
The chapter that declares a type knows how to find it, so it also registers
a function that fetches an example, in a chunk [[<<example fetchers>>]]:
\begin{verbatim}
@example("lti/placement")
def placement_of_the_launched_activity(fbf, login):
    ...
    return placement
\end{verbatim}
A fetcher gets the test's [[FBF]] client and, as [[login]], the
[[FBFLogin]] of the launch, whose [[course_uuid]] and [[activity_uuid]]
say where to start looking, and returns one resource.
It runs in a service test, so its requests are recorded and replayed like
any other.

The registry is a dictionary from type to fetcher.
Registering a type twice is a mistake, likely a copied chunk, and we refuse
it rather than let the later fetcher silently win.
<<example registry>>=
EXAMPLES = {}


def example(kind):
    """Register the decorated function as the fetcher of an example `kind`."""
    def register(fetch):
        if kind in EXAMPLES:
            raise ValueError(f"two example fetchers for {kind!r}")
        EXAMPLES[kind] = fetch
        return fetch
    return register
@

The chunk starts empty here; the chapters add their fetchers.
<<example fetchers>>=
# The example fetchers of the chapters follow.
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_example_refuses_a_second_fetcher_for_a_type(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(conftest, "EXAMPLES", {})
  conftest.example("test-type")(lambda fbf, login: None)
  with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="test-type"):
    conftest.example("test-type")(lambda fbf, login: None)
