\section{Recording your own cassettes}
\label{RecordingOwn}

To record, we need a Canvas login through [[canvaslms login]], with access to
the course and assignment of [[CANVAS_URL]], [[COURSE_ID]] and
[[ASSIGNMENT_ID]], and a pseudonymization key.
Any long random string will do as a key, but it must stay the same between
recordings and it must never be written to the repository or printed.
With GNOME Keyring we can create one without ever seeing it:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32), end='')" \
  | secret-tool store --label='fbf test pseudonymization key' \
      service fbf key test-pseudonym
\end{verbatim}
Then we record the cassettes that are missing, reading the key from the
keyring for this command only:
\begin{verbatim}
TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY="$(secret-tool lookup service fbf key test-pseudonym)" \
  make test PYTESTFLAGS=--record-mode=once
\end{verbatim}
A live run takes the key the same way, with [[PYTESTFLAGS=--live]].
The recording fails if the scan afterwards finds personal data
(\cref{Drift}); then extend the key lists in \cref{Pseudonymization},
delete the cassettes and record again.
Before trusting new cassettes, scan them once more with the [[testing]]
skill's [[scan_leaks.py]], which also takes a list of names to look for.
